//! FlashInfer's kernels, ported to cute-rs.
//!
//! [FlashInfer](https://github.com/flashinfer-ai/flashinfer) (Apache-2.0) is the kernel
//! library behind vLLM's and SGLang's attention, sampling and normalisation. This crate
//! rebuilds its kernels with cute-rs: each one is built at run time, checked on cute-rs's
//! race-checking simulator against an f64 reference, and on a GPU (`--features cuda`)
//! checked again and benchmarked against FlashInfer itself.
//!
//! One module per FlashInfer kernel family, named after FlashInfer's Python module:
//!
//! | Module | FlashInfer | Status |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | [`decode`] | `BatchDecodeWithPagedKVCacheWrapper` (`fa2`), its split-KV plan and `merge_states` ; `single_decode_with_kv_cache` (contiguous K/V, FlashInfer's single-decode split) | f16 or bf16 q/cache/output, head dim 64/128/256 (bf16 and 64/256 at FlashInfer's speed, bandwidth-bound at ~205-210 GB/s), GQA group 4, NHD; within 10% of FlashInfer on an RTX 2000 Ada; single decode 2-5% faster at kv_len 1K-32K; FP8 (E4M3/E5M2) K/V cache with `k_scale`/`v_scale` (build-time `KvDtype`), 3-24% faster than FlashInfer's FP8 decode on its benchmark shapes; build-time variants (soft-cap, sliding window that skips out-of-window tokens, ALiBi): soft-cap at FlashInfer's speed, window 1024 at kv 4096 3.9x faster |
//! | [`norm`] | `rmsnorm`, `fused_add_rmsnorm`, `gemma_rmsnorm`, `gemma_fused_add_rmsnorm`, `layernorm` (f32 gamma/beta), `rmsnorm_quant` / `fused_add_rmsnorm_quant` (E4M3 out, scale buffer), `rmsnorm` of 3-D input (strided QK head norm) | f16/bf16; as fast as FlashInfer's `norm.cuh`, and within 14% of its default CuTe DSL kernel on every measured shape; the Gemma and layer-norm variants within 15% (0.1-0.8 us, near the launch floor) on the same shapes, and faster on most; the quant variants within ~15% of FlashInfer (fused-add 989x3072 f16 19.2 us against 21.5), the 3-D QK norm 0-12% faster from 99 tokens and 0.1-0.25 us slower at 1-19 (no E5M2 output, `layernorm_quant`, block-quant, SiLU, DiT or RoPE-fused norms) |
//! | [`rope`] | `apply_rope_with_cos_sin_cache_inplace`, `apply_rope[_pos_ids]_inplace`, `apply_llama31_rope[_pos_ids]_inplace` (`rotary_dim <= head_dim`; `(indptr, offsets)` via host-expanded positions), `append_paged_kv_cache` | f16/bf16, NeoX and GPT-J, head dim 64/128; NHD append, also into an FP8 (E4M3/E5M2) cache from FP8 inputs as FlashInfer's; the frequency forms match FlashInfer at 1-16 tokens, are 1.5x faster on its neox kernel at 512 and equal when bandwidth-bound; Llama 3.1 neox at 512 tokens is 20-30% slower |
//! | [`quantization`] | `packbits`, `segment_packbits`, `per_token_group_quant_8bit` (group 64/128/256, f16/bf16 to E4M3/E5M2/int8, row- or column-major scales, `scale_ue8m0`, bit-exact; the MXFP8/NVFP4 quantizers, SM100-only, and the Hopper/Blackwell-only `prepare_fp8_batched_gemm` not ported) | both bit orders, i32 indptr; at FlashInfer's speed or faster on all but two small shapes (0.2-0.8 us slower, at the launch floor) |
//! | [`sampling`] | `sampling_from_probs`, `top_k`/`top_p`/`top_k_top_p`/`min_p_sampling_from_probs` (joint), `top_p_renorm_probs`, `top_k_renorm_probs`, `top_k_mask_logits`, `softmax` with temperature, `top_k` (values and indices), `chain_speculative_sampling`, `sampling_from_logits` (Gumbel-max), `top_k_top_p_sampling_from_probs` / `_from_logits` in both filter orders (a compact top-k path for logits), per-row seed / offset arrays | f32, curand-exact Philox; sampling 0.88-1.08x FlashInfer's time on its benchmark shapes; min-p 0.92-1.01x; top-k renorm / mask and softmax faster at every batch (below 16 rows a row is split over up to 16 blocks); top-p renorm level at batch 1, faster from batch 16; `top_k` 0.5-0.72x; speculative 0.82-1.15x; `top_k_page_table_transform` / `top_k_ragged_transform` (f32 scores, `row_starts`, `row_to_batch`, page sizes 2^s, rows of any length: 0.4-1.1x FlashInfer's time at k 2048, batch 1-64, 4K-128K scores; f16/bf16 scores, `out_raw_indices`, tie-break modes and the clusters/CUB backends not ported) |
//! | [`attention`] | `BatchAttention` (persistent holistic kernel: mixed prefill + decode over a paged cache from per-CTA work lists, `TwoStageHolisticPlan`, in-kernel persistent reduction) | f16, head dim 128, GQA, causal, page size 1/16; FlashInfer's own kernel cannot launch on SM89 (cooperative grid too large); on its `bench_batch_attention.py` mixes within -4%/+5% of FlashInfer's batch prefill and decode + prefill on an RTX 2000 Ada (30.1 vs 31.2 ms on the default mix), 25% behind POD (FlashInfer's task order serialises the compute- and bandwidth-bound halves; an alternate order gets 28.7 ms) |
//! | [`pod`] | `PODWithPagedKVCacheWrapper`: a (single) prefill and a paged decode batch in one launch, CTAs split between them by FlashInfer's SM-aware scheduler | f16, head dim 128, GQA, causal prefill; built from the [`prefill`] kernel's CTA body (tile 128/64/16 prefill, 16-row tensor-core decode); on an RTX 2000 Ada at ~200 GB/s on FlashInfer's `bench_mixed_attention.py` shapes, 1-18% faster than the same halves in two launches (idle-GPU rerun; the earlier 6000/7000 row was contaminated), and within 1-2.5% of FlashInfer's own POD timed with CUDA events (its graph replay skips the counter memset and does no work) |
//! | [`prefill`] | `BatchPrefillWithPagedKVCacheWrapper` (`fa2`), its planner and split-KV merge; `BatchPrefillWithRaggedKVCacheWrapper` (`fa2`) and `single_prefill_with_kv_cache` on the same kernel | f16 or bf16 q/K/V/O, head dim 64/128/256 (256 uses tiles 64/16 only, as FlashInfer), GQA, causal or not; CTA tiles 128/64/16; paged within 1.3% of FlashInfer or faster (up to 38.6 TFLOPS on an RTX 2000 Ada); ragged and single within 1% or faster from 2K tokens, 7% slower at 512-1K; build-time variants (soft-cap, sliding window, packed custom mask, ALiBi), causal + window 1024 at FlashInfer's speed; FP8 (E4M3/E5M2) KV cache with `k_scale`/`v_scale` and f16 queries, paged/ragged/single, 0-20% faster than FlashInfer's FP8 prefill |
//! | [`activation`] | `silu_and_mul`, `gelu_and_mul`, `gelu_tanh_and_mul` | f16/bf16, 16-byte vectors, FlashInfer's split-row launch plan; at FlashInfer's speed on large batches (bandwidth-bound), `gelu` faster, within ~10-20% on one-token launches (about 0.2 us) |
//! | [`cascade`] | `merge_state`, `merge_state_in_place`, `merge_states` f16/bf16, head dim 64/128/256, simple and cp.async kernels; at FlashInfer's speed when bandwidth-bound, 10-30% (0.3-0.9 us) slower on L2-resident small shapes |
//! | [`mla`] | `BatchMLAPagedAttentionWrapper` (`fa2`), its planner and persistent merge | f16, head dims 512 + 64, any head count, causal or not; 12-23% faster than FlashInfer on its DeepSeek MLA benchmark shapes |
//! | [`moe`] | `cutlass_fused_moe` (SM80/SM89 path: permute, fused FC1 + SwiGLU grouped GEMM, FC2 grouped GEMM, finalize) with precomputed `token_selected_experts` / `token_final_scales` | f16/bf16, SwiGLU, no EP/TP, biases or quantization; on an RTX 2000 Ada at FlashInfer's speed (within 2%) when weight-bandwidth-bound and up to 2.9x faster elsewhere on Mixtral 8x7B and FlashInfer's `bench_cutlass_fused_moe.py` shapes (1-1024 tokens); routing from logits: softmax-top-k (optionally renormalized), Renormalize, DeepSeek-V3 grouped (`fused_topk_deepseek`, 1.3-3.2x faster than FlashInfer's at 1-8192 tokens), Llama4, TopK, Sigmoid, SigmoidRenorm, MiniMax2 and TopKSigmoid |
//! | [`gemm`] | `SegmentGEMMWrapper.run` (SM80 CUTLASS path), `bmm_fp8` (cuBLASLt on SM89) | segment GEMM f16/bf16, row- or column-major weights, optional `weight_indices`; `bmm_fp8` E4M3/E5M2 to bf16/f16; on an RTX 2000 Ada the segment GEMM is 2-8x faster than FlashInfer's CUTLASS path (up to 38 TFLOPS; FlashInfer launches four CTAs), and `bmm_fp8` matches cuBLASLt at m = 4 and is 6% slower at m = 64 |
//! | [`mamba`] | `selective_state_update`, single-token and multi-token (FlashInfer's simple kernel) | f16/bf16 inputs, f16/bf16/f32 state, f32 `dt`/`A`/`D`/`dt_bias`; optional `z`, `D`, `dt_bias`, softplus, `state_batch_indices` with pad slot, `dst_state_batch_indices`, varlen `cu_seqlens`, `num_accepted_tokens`, the MTP intermediate-state cache, int16 block-scaled state (batch 16, 128 heads: within 1% of FlashInfer on dst, varlen, accepted tokens and int16, 2% slower on the 6-token cache, equal on 6-token dst); SM90 TMA kernels and Philox rounding not ported; at FlashInfer's speed or faster, MTP at batch 1 included (10.7 us against 14.0 us, 6 tokens) |
//! | [`gdn`] | `gated_delta_rule_decode_pretranspose` (single-token Gated Delta Net decode), `gated_delta_rule_mtp` (multi-token, with the intermediate-state cache and `disable_state_update`) and `chunk_gated_delta_rule` (chunked prefill, after FlashInfer's SM80 CuTe DSL kernel: 64-token chunks, `mma.sync`, blockwise triangular solve); decode kernels redesigned for SM80+ | f16/bf16 q/k/v, f32/bf16/f16 `[V, K]` state, GVA, optional l2norm, state pool with `output_state_indices` and padding; on an RTX 2000 Ada within 1% of FlashInfer on large batches and 1.1-2.1x faster at batch 1 (T=1 bf16 state, MTP T=2-4), 7% faster at batch 16 T=4 verify; FlashInfer's cached-verify path fails on SM89, so the cache is measured against bandwidth (~90% of peak); non-transposed `[K, V]` state (`kv_layout`, any state dtype, T >= 1, pools; f32 state, K = 128, batch 1-4: a tile kernel after FlashInfer's, V-contiguous `cp.async` tiles, 1.3x faster than `gated_delta_rule_decode` (8.1 vs 10.5 us, 27.3 vs 35.2 us); 1.6-2.8% slower at batch 16-64 where the DRAM-bound column-vector kernel runs) and the per-token pool scatter (`ssm_state_indices`, T >= 2; 1.1-1.7x faster than FlashInfer at T=4); tree verify and ReplaySSM not ported; prefill (varlen, K = 128, initial/final state, pools, l2norm) 1.0-1.16x FlashInfer's speed at 1k-16k tokens on Qwen3-Next heads, GQA, context parallelism, checkpoints and FP8 state not ported |
//! | [`kda`] | `recurrent_kda` / KDA decode (Kimi Delta Attention: GDN with a per-channel gate), single- and multi-token | f16/bf16 inputs, f16/bf16/f32 state, softplus or lower-bound sigmoid gates, optional `dt_bias` and l2norm, GVA, state pool with padding, per-token checkpoints; bandwidth-bound (~204 GB/s T=1, ~220 GB/s T=4) at batch 64+; on SM89 equal to FlashInfer's `recurrent_kda` at T=1 (+-1%) and 5-12% faster at T=4; packed `cu_seqlens` (also T=1) and `num_accepted_tokens` (0.94-1.06x FlashInfer's time at T=1, 1.0-1.1x faster at T=4 on a 16/32-head, batch 1-64 sweep); the frozen-verify caches not ported |
//! | [`sparse`] | `BlockSparseAttentionWrapper` (BSR, any `R x C`, optional custom mask) and `VariableBlockSparseAttentionWrapper` (per-head block sizes and patterns), `fa2` path | f16, head dim 64/128, GQA, not causal; a paged prefill on the [`prefill`] kernel's CTA body; on an RTX 2000 Ada within 2% of FlashInfer on 4K-16K BSR shapes (64 x 64 blocks, 50% / 90% sparse, head dim 64 and 128) and its variable-block benchmark, except variable 8K at density 0.1 (9% slower); with a custom mask 1.46x faster; FlashInfer's CUDA-core path for `R * group < 4` not ported |
//! | [`xqa`] | `xqa` (TensorRT-LLM's XQA GQA decode, SM80/SM89 `mha.cu` path): all query heads of a KV head in one 16-row tensor-core tile, paged cache, multi-block split merged in-kernel by the last CTA | f16/bf16 q, f16/bf16 or E4M3 (`kv_scale`) cache, `q_scale`, page 16-128, group 1-16, head dim 128, sliding window, attention sinks; on an RTX 2000 Ada (CUDA graphs) 0.5-2% faster than FlashInfer's own XQA built for sm_89 on f16 bandwidth-bound shapes (~210 GB/s) and 13-20% faster at batch 1 kv 1-4K, and at or ahead of `decode` |
//!
